Remark 5.3 a) By a direct check using Theorem 5.1 one finds that L(64, 9)
                                                                               sl (C)
and L(64, 25) are distinguished by the sl4 (C)–invariant, in fact by τ6 4 . On
the other hand these lens spaces can not be distinguished by the LMO invariant.
This follows by [10, Proposition 5.1] and the fact that S(9/64) = S(25/64)(=
−63/32), cf. [30, Remark (4.14)]. Since the perturbative invariant τ P sln(C)
[36] can be recovered from the LMO invariant, cf. [41], we see that τ P sln(C)
does not separate L(64, 9) and L(64, 25). The perturbative invariant τ P sln(C)
                                                                       P sl (C)
is determined by the family of quantum P sln (C)–invariants τr n , see [36].
Oppositely it is expected that the perturbative invariant τ P sln(C) dominates
                   P sl (C)
the invariants τr n , cf. [36, Conjecture 1.8]. From the explicit formulas
                             P sl (C)
for the P sl4 –invariants τr 4        of the lens spaces in [51], it follows that these
invariants can not distinguish between L(64, 9) and L(64, 25) for r a prime. It
would be interesting to examine if this is also the case for the non-prime r for
